layout: links
link: Microsoft, cloud-based ransomware (2025) | https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/security/blog/2025/08/27/storm-0501s-evolving-techniques-lead-to-cloud-based-ransomware/
link: Microsoft, hybrid cloud attacks (2024) | https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/security/blog/2024/09/26/storm-0501-ransomware-attacks-expanding-to-hybrid-cloud-environments/
link: ASD's ACSC, CISA, NSA and partners: AD compromises | https://www.cyber.gov.au/publication/detecting-and-mitigating-active-directory-compromises
link: Microsoft IR, Octo Tempest recovery | https://techcommunity.microsoft.com/blog/microsoftsecurityexperts/octo-tempest-hybrid-identity-compromise-recovery/4166783
link: Tenable, Entra ID synchronization | https://www.tenable.com/blog/despite-recent-security-hardening-entra-id-synchronization-feature-remains-open-for-abuse
link: Microsoft Learn, immutable blob storage | https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/blobs/immutable-storage-overview
link: Microsoft Learn, Key Vault soft-delete | https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/key-vault/general/soft-delete-overview
link: Dark Reading, Storm-0501 | https://www.darkreading.com/cloud-security/storm-0501-cloud-based-ransomware-attack
notes:
These are the primary sources the csoh.org kill chain cites. Every fact about the incident in this deck comes from that page, which draws on these.

Microsoft Threat Intelligence's 2025 post is the account of this intrusion; its 2024 post covers earlier Storm-0501 victims. The guide from ASD's ACSC, CISA, NSA and partners covers Entra Connect and synced privileged accounts, and Microsoft Incident Response's Octo Tempest playbook covers eviction. Tenable covers the sync account's role. The Microsoft Learn pages cover immutability policies and Key Vault soft delete. Dark Reading noted that Microsoft did not say whether the victim paid.

The page also cites Microsoft Learn on elevate access, immutable vaults for Azure Backup, encryption scopes and the Entra Connect version history, Microsoft Graph on federatedTokenValidationPolicy, and MITRE ATT&CK's pages for Storm-0501, group G1053, and four of the chain's techniques. They are left off this slide for space.

The background slides name their own sources, MITRE ATT&CK and Microsoft Learn.

== Related kill chains
layout: links
link: Storm-2949 | breaches/storm-2949-entra-sspr.html
link: Storm-3168 | breaches/storm-3168-jadepuffer-azure.html
link: Codefinger | breaches/codefinger-s3.html
link: SolarWinds | breaches/solarwinds.html
The page sets each of these beside a stage of this chain.
notes:
The page links four other kill chains, each beside the stage it compares.

At stage 07 it compares Storm-2949, where the compromised identities already held privileged Azure RBAC; here an Entra role became Azure Owner through two operations.

At stages 08 and 09 it cites Storm-3168: Microsoft noted there that the same key collection could enable future data access, and there attempts to remove recovery locks failed.

At stage 10 it compares Codefinger, which used the same idea in S3, encrypting with keys AWS never stores.

At stage 06 it notes that the SolarWinds intruders also added trusted federated identity providers in Azure AD.

The page's related defense topics are Azure security, IAM and cloud identity, non-human identity, backup and disaster recovery, detection engineering, and incident response.
